{"parentUuid":"91ed573b-f893-4035-8017-1f847daaa9f2","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_016ng6wc9YbUzSVUbfLjxNQb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01LMfCXLo7iMHKV6Xiuy588F","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"for pdf in /root/papers/all/2407.12036v2.pdf /root/papers/all/2408.08127v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2408.12633v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2409.11272v7.pdf /root/papers/all/2409.15949v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2410.03129v2.pdf /root/papers/all/2411.04734v2.pdf /root/papers/all/2411.16408v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2501.07557v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2502.21321v2.pdf /root/papers/all/2503.12340v1.pdf /root/papers/all/2505.00035v1.pdf; do\n  echo \"=== $(basename $pdf) ===\"\n  pdftotext \"$pdf\" - 2>/dev/null | head -100 || strings \"$pdf\" | head -100\n  echo \"\"\ndone","description":"Extract text from PDFs using pdftotext"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5371,"cache_read_input_tokens":122937,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5371,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXbDAiefKwHfpSEiN2phe","type":"assistant","uuid":"e9706b42-2256-42ce-96fa-870653226664","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:37.185Z"}
{"parentUuid":"e9706b42-2256-42ce-96fa-870653226664","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01LMfCXLo7iMHKV6Xiuy588F","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (67KB). Full output saved to: /logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01LMfCXLo7iMHKV6Xiuy588F.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n=== 2407.12036v2.pdf ===\nE XPLORING A DVANCED L ARGE L ANGUAGE M ODELS WITH\nLLMS UITE\n\narXiv:2407.12036v2 [cs.CL] 12 Nov 2024\n\nA Free Comprehensive Recap of Techniques, Architectures, and Practical Applications\n\nGiorgio Roffo\nSr. Research Scientist\n{giorgio.roffo}@gmail.com\n\nA BSTRACT\nThis tutorial explores the advancements and challenges in the development of Large Language\nModels (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and Gemini. It addresses inherent limitations like temporal\nknowledge cutoffs, mathematical inaccuracies, and the generation of incorrect information, proposing\nsolutions like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), Program-Aided Language Models (PAL),\nand frameworks such as ReAct and LangChain. The integration of these techniques enhances LLM\nperformance and reliability, especially in multi-step reasoning and complex task execution. The\npaper also covers fine-tuning strategies, including instruction fine-tuning, parameter-efficient methods\nlike LoRA, and Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) as well as Reinforced\nSelf-Training (ReST). Additionally, it provides a comprehensive survey of transformer architectures\nand training techniques for LLMs. The source code can be accessed by contacting the author via\nemail for a request.\nKeywords Large Language Models · RAG · CoT · PAL · ReAct · ZeRO · FSDP · PEFT · LoRA · RLHF · ReST · PPO\n\n1\n\nIntroduction\n\nIn recent years, the field of natural language processing (NLP) has witnessed groundbreaking advancements with\nthe development and deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and Gemini. These models,\ncharacterized by their ability to generate human-like text, have set new benchmarks in a variety of applications, from\nautomated customer support to creative writing. Despite their impressive capabilities, LLMs are not without limitations.\nThey often struggle with the temporal limitations of their knowledge base, complex mathematical computations, and a\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"235e1fe8-cedc-4d83-8b94-d855fd2a8d94","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:39.272Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e9706b42-2256-42ce-96fa-870653226664"}
{"parentUuid":"235e1fe8-cedc-4d83-8b94-d855fd2a8d94","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_016W4ks1FYuBBo4jf55Vhyu7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01WKJUksTHXLAk4sXDjGQRUu","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01LMfCXLo7iMHKV6Xiuy588F.txt","limit":800}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":993,"cache_read_input_tokens":128308,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":993,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":25}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXbDBQt6sDg1ZJ4xYgkzo","type":"assistant","uuid":"b7da0da4-bc86-4a6b-bcee-5ff8819cc03c","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:42.196Z"}
{"parentUuid":"b7da0da4-bc86-4a6b-bcee-5ff8819cc03c","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01WKJUksTHXLAk4sXDjGQRUu","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→=== 2407.12036v2.pdf ===\n     2→E XPLORING A DVANCED L ARGE L ANGUAGE M ODELS WITH\n     3→LLMS UITE\n     4→\n     5→arXiv:2407.12036v2 [cs.CL] 12 Nov 2024\n     6→\n     7→A Free Comprehensive Recap of Techniques, Architectures, and Practical Applications\n     8→\n     9→Giorgio Roffo\n    10→Sr. Research Scientist\n    11→{giorgio.roffo}@gmail.com\n    12→\n    13→A BSTRACT\n    14→This tutorial explores the advancements and challenges in the development of Large Language\n    15→Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and Gemini. It addresses inherent limitations like temporal\n    16→knowledge cutoffs, mathematical inaccuracies, and the generation of incorrect information, proposing\n    17→solutions like Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), Program-Aided Language Models (PAL),\n    18→and frameworks such as ReAct and LangChain. The integration of these techniques enhances LLM\n    19→performance and reliability, especially in multi-step reasoning and complex task execution. The\n    20→paper also covers fine-tuning strategies, including instruction fine-tuning, parameter-efficient methods\n    21→like LoRA, and Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) as well as Reinforced\n    22→Self-Training (ReST). Additionally, it provides a comprehensive survey of transformer architectures\n    23→and training techniques for LLMs. The source code can be accessed by contacting the author via\n    24→email for a request.\n    25→Keywords Large Language Models · RAG · CoT · PAL · ReAct · ZeRO · FSDP · PEFT · LoRA · RLHF · ReST · PPO\n    26→\n    27→1\n    28→\n    29→Introduction\n    30→\n    31→In recent years, the field of natural language processing (NLP) has witnessed groundbreaking advancements with\n    32→the development and deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT and Gemini. These models,\n    33→characterized by their ability to generate human-like text, have set new benchmarks in a variety of applications, from\n    34→automated customer support to creative writing. Despite their impressive capabilities, LLMs are not without limitations.\n    35→They often struggle with the temporal limitations of their knowledge base, complex mathematical computations, and a\n    36→tendency to produce plausible but incorrect information—commonly referred to as \"hallucinations\".\n    37→Addressing these limitations has become a focal point of contemporary research. One promising approach is the\n    38→integration of LLMs with external data sources and applications, which can significantly enhance their accuracy\n    39→and relevance without the need for costly retraining. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) [23] is a prominent\n    40→technique in this regard, augmenting LLMs by linking them to up-to-date external databases to improve the precision of\n    41→their outputs. Additionally, advanced prompting strategies like chain-of-thought prompting [47] aid in improving the\n    42→reasoning capabilities of LLMs, especially for tasks requiring multi-step logic.\n    43→Moreover, specialized frameworks such as Program-Aided Language Models (PAL) [12] and ReAct [50] have been\n    44→developed to extend the functionalities of LLMs. PAL leverages external code interpreters to handle precise mathematical\n    45→computations, while ReAct integrates reasoning with action planning to manage complex workflows. LangChain [42],\n    46→another significant development, offers modular components and agents to facilitate the integration of LLMs into\n    47→diverse applications, enhancing their ability to execute complex tasks efficiently.\n    48→This tutorial paper1 provides a comprehensive exploration of the challenges and solutions associated with LLMs. It\n    49→begins by examining the inherent limitations of LLMs, such as temporal knowledge cutoffs, mathematical inaccuracies,\n    50→1\n    51→\n    52→Special thanks to Shelbee Eigenbrode, Antje Barth, and Mike Chambers for their work on \"Generative AI with Large Language\n    53→Models\" (Amazon AWS, 2023) [1], which significantly informed and inspired the material used in this document.\n    54→\n    55→\fG. Roffo - Introduction to Advanced Large Language Models\n    56→\n    57→and hallucinations. The paper then introduces Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) as a means to access realtime external information, thus improving LLM performance across various applications. For instance, in customer\n    58→service bots, RAG can enable real-time interactions with databases and APIs, enhancing the relevance and accuracy of\n    59→responses.\n    60→The paper also discusses the integration of LLMs with external applications to perform complex tasks. Additionally, the\n    61→concept of chain-of-thought prompting is introduced to enhance the reasoning capabilities of LLMs in multi-step tasks.\n    62→This approach encourages the model to break down complex problems into intermediate steps, improving the coherence\n    63→and logic of the generated responses.\n    64→Further, the paper delves into the Program-Aided Language Model (PAL) framework [12], which pairs LLMs with\n    65→external code interpreters to execute accurate calculations. This integration is crucial for improving the mathematical\n    66→capabilities of LLMs, allowing them to handle tasks that require precise numerical computations. Recent advancements\n    67→such as the ReAct [50] framework and LangChain [42] are also explored, highlighting their potential in guiding LLMs\n    68→through structured prompts to solve intricate problems and support advanced techniques like PAL.\n    69→The architectural components necessary for developing LLM-powered applications are thoroughly outlined, covering\n    70→aspects such as infrastructure, deployment, and the integration of external information sources. The paper reviews\n    71→various transformer-based models and recent advancements aimed at enhancing performance and efficiency. Techniques\n    72→for scaling model training beyond a single GPU, including PyTorch’s Distributed Data Parallel (DDP) and Fully Sharded\n    73→Data Parallel (FSDP) [51], are discussed, along with the ZeRO [33] stages that optimize memory usage during training.\n    74→Fine-tuning strategies are a significant focus, exploring methods to enhance LLM performance for specific use cases.\n    75→The paper examines instruction fine-tuning, multitask fine-tuning, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) methods\n    76→like Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) [18] and prompt tuning [20]. These techniques address the issue of catastrophic\n    77→forgetting and suggest ways to mitigate it. Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) [52, 13] and\n    78→Reinforced Self-Training [15] are also explored as a method to align LLMs with human preferences. The use of\n    79→Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) [35] to update LLM weights based on human evaluations is discussed, along\n    80→with challenges like reward hacking and the importance of maintaining model quality through techniques such as KL\n    81→divergence.\n    82→Finally, the paper provides an introduction to PPO [35], its phases, and its objectives, including policy loss, value\n    83→function loss, and entropy loss. It discusses how PPO ensures stable learning by constraining updates within a trust\n    84→region and its application in fine-tuning LLMs for human alignment. The integration of external data sources and the\n    85→application of advanced frameworks and fine-tuning strategies significantly enhance the performance and reliability of\n    86→LLMs, offering an overview of the current state and future directions in LLM-based applications.\n    87→The toolbox includes not only the code but also comprehensive tutorial slides to aid understanding and application. For\n    88→fine-tuning models, we often need multi-task settings and diverse datasets to ensure robust performance across various\n    89→tasks. Having references to the available datasets is crucial for researchers and practitioners in selecting appropriate\n    90→data for their specific fine-tuning needs. Table 1 presents an overview of datasets and benchmarks commonly used in\n    91→natural language processing tasks, including their type, citation, and a brief description. For additional resources and\n    92→information, please contact or visit the Author Page or G. Roffo. The source code can be accessed by contacting the\n    93→author via email for a request.\n    94→In summary, this report serves as a guide to the techniques, architectures, and practical applications of Large Language\n    95→Models. By addressing the inherent limitations of LLMs and introducing innovative frameworks and strategies, it aims\n    96→to enhance the capabilities and reliability of these powerful tools in various real-world applications.\n    97→\n    98→2\n    99→\n   100→Beyond Basic LLMs\n   101→\n   102→\n   103→=== 2408.08127v1.pdf ===\n   104→The evolution of inharmonicity and noisiness in contemporary\n   105→popular music\n   106→Emmanuel Derutya,b , David Meredithb , and Stefan Lattnera\n   107→a\n   108→\n   109→Sony Computer Science Laboratories, 6 rue Amyot, 75005 Paris, France\n   110→Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Aalborg University,\n   111→Rendsburggade 14, 9000 Aalborg, Denmark\n   112→\n   113→arXiv:2408.08127v1 [cs.SD] 15 Aug 2024\n   114→\n   115→b\n   116→\n   117→ARTICLE HISTORY\n   118→Compiled August 16, 2024\n   119→ABSTRACT\n   120→Much of Western classical music uses instruments based on acoustic resonance. Such\n   121→instruments produce harmonic or quasi-harmonic sounds. On the other hand, since\n   122→the early 1970s, popular music has largely been produced in the recording studio.\n   123→As a result, popular music is not bound to be based on harmonic or quasi-harmonic\n   124→sounds. In this study, we use modified MPEG-7 features to explore and characterise the way in which the use of noise and inharmonicity has evolved in popular\n   125→music since 1961. We set this evolution in the context of other broad categories of\n   126→music, including Western classical piano music, Western classical orchestral music,\n   127→and musique concrète. We propose new features that allow us to distinguish between\n   128→inharmonicity resulting from noise and inharmonicity resulting from interactions between relatively discrete partials. When the history of contemporary popular music\n   129→is viewed through the lens of these new features, we find that the period since 1961\n   130→can be divided into three phases. From 1961 to 1972, there was a steady increase in\n   131→inharmonicity but no significant increase in noise. From 1972 to 1986, both inharmonicity and noise increased. Then, since 1986, there has been a steady decrease\n   132→in both inharmonicity and noise to today’s popular music which is significantly less\n   133→noisy but more inharmonic than the music of the sixties. We relate these observed\n   134→trends to the development of music production practice over the period and illustrate\n   135→them with focused analyses of certain key artists and tracks.\n   136→KEYWORDS\n   137→Popular music; inharmonicity; diachronic music analysis; noisiness; HarmonicRatio\n   138→\n   139→CONTACT Emmanuel Deruty. Email: emmanuel.deruty@sony.com\n   140→\n   141→\f1. Introduction\n   142→Most musical instruments that perform Western classical music are based on the acoustic resonance principle. Such instruments produce harmonic or quasi-harmonic complex tones. In contemporary popular music, the means of production are more diversified. Drums play a key role, and they do not typically produce quasi-harmonic complex\n   143→tones. Instruments may be based on electronic or digital sound production techniques.\n   144→The instruments’ outputs may be heavily processed with effects. Musicians may not\n   145→even use instruments and produce the entirety of the music on a computer. As a result,\n   146→given a contemporary popular music track, it is possible that the description of the\n   147→corresponding signal using quasi-harmonic complex tones is unsuitable.\n   148→In the spectral domain, a quasi-harmonic complex tone may be described as a\n   149→series of partials whose frequency values are near multiples of one frequency value\n   150→(the fundamental). There are at least two ways audio content may diverge from being\n   151→quasi-harmonic: as exemplified in Lavengood (2017, p. 22), the energy may not be\n   152→centered around partials, in which case the signal may be described as ‘noisy’; or,\n   153→as exemplified in Lavengood (2017, p. 24), the partial frequency values may not be\n   154→harmonically related, in which case the tone may be described as ‘inharmonic’.\n   155→In this paper, we trace the evolution over time of the relative amounts of inharmonicity and noise in various audio datasets. Our main focus is contemporary popular\n   156→music (CPM), but we also compare this class of music with Western classical piano\n   157→music, Western orchestral music, and music concrète.\n   158→One key conclusion of this study is that contemporary popular music is more inharmonic than the music from the three other datasets considered. Our diachronic\n   159→analysis of the evolution of noisiness and inharmonicity in CPM leads to the conclusion that, between 1961 and 1986, recorded music evolved in a general direction that\n   160→goes from acoustic, tonal instruments playing content from a musical score (or, at\n   161→least, that can be transcribed to a score), to heavily processed, noisy, and inharmonic\n   162→content made in the recording studio and including drums. In more recent decades,\n   163→music has become slightly more harmonic.\n   164→The remainder of this paper is structured as follows. The core content of the study\n   165→can be found in sections 3 and 4 (the ‘core sections’). Section 3 describes the datasets\n   166→and the signal features used in the study. Section 4 presents the results of the analysis.\n   167→Sections 5 to 11 elaborate on several notions introduced in the core sections. Section 5\n   168→reflects on the relationship between the means of production and the produced music.\n   169→It elaborates on the reasoning that led to examining noise and inharmonicity in contemporary popular music. Section 6 details characteristics of the signal that influence\n   170→the inharmonic character of the music, discusses the link between inharmonicity and\n   171→acoustic beating (in other words the acoustical ‘grit’), and substantiates the definition\n   172→of inharmonicity we use in the core sections. Section 7 details the reasoning leading to\n   173→the definition of ‘noisiness’ we use in the core sections. Section 8 addresses the influence of the recording medium and remastering processes. Section 9 relates the results\n   174→from the core sections to those of previous diachronic studies. Section 10 details the\n   175→influence of the psychoacoustic weighting used in the core sections. Section 11 presents\n   176→detailed analyses of specific music tracks from the perspective proposed in the core\n   177→sections. The paper concludes by summarising the findings from all sections.\n   178→\n   179→2\n   180→\n   181→\f2. Background and motivation\n   182→A defining property of a harmonic tone is that it has a fundamental frequency or\n   183→f0 . A classic music information retrieval task consists in estimating the fundamental\n   184→frequency of a sound, often taken as a synonym for ‘pitch tracking’ or ‘pitch estimation’\n   185→(Kim et al., 2018; Riou et al., 2023). The models performing such tasks are trained\n   186→on harmonic or quasi-harmonic audio. For instance, Riou et al. (2023) uses MIR-1K,\n   187→a dataset of quasi-harmonic samples (Hsu and Jang, 2009) and MDB-stem-synth, a\n   188→dataset of ‘exact multiples of the f0 ’ (Salamon et al., 2017).\n   189→Another classic music information retrieval task is audio-to-MIDI alignment (Raffel\n   190→and Ellis, 2016), in which MIDI files are aligned to a corresponding audio content.\n   191→MIDI files have been referred to as symbolic ‘versions’ of pieces of music (Ewert et al.,\n   192→2012; Raffel and Ellis, 2016), a ‘transcription’ (Benetos et al., 2018; Raffel, 2016;\n   193→Turetsky and Ellis, 2003) of the pitched content of the music. Such content has been\n   194→characterised as ‘harmonic’ (Ono et al., 2008) and likely to being played with ‘harmonic\n   195→instruments’ (Ewert et al., 2014).\n   196→In the pitch tracking and audio-to-midi alignment tasks, pitched content is generally\n   197→considered harmonic or quasi-harmonic. Yet, interaction with actual professional music\n   198→production may show a different picture. For example, Figure 1 shows the power\n   199→spectrum of one bass ‘note’ in the 2023 track ‘¡Fire!’ by Primaal.1 Primaal is a brand\n   200→of the Hyper Music production company mentioned by Deruty et al. (2022). In 2022\n   201→and 2023, Primaal authored music for commercials commissioned by brands such as\n   202→L’Oréal,2 Adidas,3 Vichy,4 Honda,5 GoPro,6 and Chanel.7 Therefore, it is possible to\n   203→consider that Primaal’s production reflects an existing trend in the music industry. We\n   204→\n   205→=== 2408.12633v1.pdf ===\n   206→1\n   207→\n   208→Title: Melody predominates over harmony in the\n   209→evolution of musical scales across 96 countries\n   210→Authors: John M McBride1, Elizabeth Phillips2, Patrick E Savage3,4, Steven Brown2, Tsvi Tlusty1,5\n   211→Affiliations: 1Center for Algorithmic and Robotized Synthesis, Institute for Basic Science, South Korea\n   212→2\n   213→McMaster University, Canada\n   214→3\n   215→University of Auckland, New Zealand\n   216→4\n   217→Keio University, Japan\n   218→5\n   219→Departments of Physics and Chemistry, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology, South Korea\n   220→\n   221→Abstract:\n   222→The standard theory of musical scales since antiquity has been based on harmony, rather than melody. Some recent\n   223→analyses support either view, and we lack a comparative test on cross-cultural data. We address this longstanding\n   224→problem through a rigorous, computational comparison of the main theories against 1,314 scales from 96 countries.\n   225→There is near-universal support for melodic theories, which predict step-sizes of 1-3 semitones. Harmony accounts\n   226→for the prevalence of some simple-integer-ratio intervals, particularly for music-theoretic scales from Eurasian\n   227→societies, which may explain their dominance amongst Western scholars. However, harmony poorly predicts scales\n   228→measured from ethnographic recordings, particularly outside of Eurasia. Overall, we show that the historical\n   229→emphasis on harmony is misguided and that melody is the primary determinant of the world’s musical scales.\n   230→\n   231→Main Text:\n   232→Scales are sets of frequency ratios, or intervals, that form the basic building blocks of melodies.1 They are found in\n   233→most music, with few exceptions (e.g., single-pitch melodies, percussion music lacking discrete pitches), and are one\n   234→of the most universal and defining features of human music.2–7 Some scales are known to be thousands of years old,\n   235→but in general scales change over time through cultural evolution.8,9 Despite cross-cultural variation, scales across\n   236→the world tend to be more similar than chance, and certain scales (e.g., the minor pentatonic) repeatedly appear in\n   237→far-flung corners of the world.10–12 This suggests that there is some selection process or conscious innovation\n   238→common to different groups of humans that leads to the use of similar scales (SI Section S1).12–23 In this study, we\n   239→compare and contrast theories of how and why scales evolved, using a diverse, global dataset of scales.\n   240→Formal written music theory is at least as old as the earliest surviving texts that describe scales mathematically from\n   241→around 400-300 B.C.E in Greece and China.24–26 The most prominent idea that has survived, albeit transformed, is\n   242→the Pythagorean theory that certain harmonic intervals are inherently consonant.27 In parallel, the Aristotelian scholar\n   243→Aristoxenus proposed a separate melodic theory based on the constraints of vocal production and perception: “The\n   244→voice cannot differentiate, nor can the ear discriminate, any interval smaller than the smallest diesis [41 cents], so as\n   245→to determine what fraction it is of a diesis or of any other of the known interval.”28 Despite their similar origins, it is\n   246→the former theory that became the dominant paradigm in Europe, to the extent that the preeminent music theorist\n   247→Jean-Phillipe Rameau claimed in 1722 that “melody is only a consequence of harmony.”29 While 21st century\n   248→computational modeling and psychoacoustic experiments have challenged the relative importance of different\n   249→aspects of harmony, they have re-affirmed the traditional view that the origin of musical scales lies in harmony,\n   250→rather than melody.13,17–19,22\n   251→\n   252→\f2\n   253→Modern theories of the innate harmonic properties of scale intervals are supported by two psychoacoustic\n   254→phenomena.30,31 Tonal fusion32 occurs when two complex tones have overlapping partials, making them difficult to\n   255→distinguish, and sensory dissonance33,34 stems from the perception of beats, which are audible interference patterns\n   256→produced by interactions between the partials of two complex tones. Despite robust empirical support for these\n   257→phenomena,15,22,34–41 we do not know how they affect scale evolution. The most common argument is that there is an\n   258→innate perceptual preference for harmonic intervals.22,31,42–45 However, the cross-cultural evidence for this is\n   259→mixed,14,46,47 and preferences may instead be a consequence of entrenched cultural exposure.39,48–51 Another theory is\n   260→that harmonization in groups is aided by tonal fusion or interference, enhancing social bonding.52 Alternatively, a\n   261→hypothesis based on instruments, rather than group musicking, is that these phenomena enable reliable tuning of\n   262→intervals (e.g., octaves and fifths) as a perceptual analogue of tuning technologies (e.g., monochords, pitch\n   263→pipes).12,53,54 Previous studies have tested various harmonicity/interference models of scale evolution,17–19,21 but they\n   264→did not compare the models among themselves, and only examined scales from a limited number of societies. In this\n   265→study, we compare multiple harmony models,31 finding that harmonicity/interference models make overlapping\n   266→predictions about harmonic tones (SI Section S2).22 We therefore focus on a representative harmonicity model\n   267→(Harmony) for simplicity (see Harmony Models).\n   268→Aristoxenus’s hypothesis was independently conceived in modern times as the Interval Spacing (IS) theory,55–60\n   269→which, together with the Motor Constraint (MC) theory,61–64 predicts that scales obey certain melodic principles. IS\n   270→theory is based on physiological limits on both vocal precision56,65–68 and interval discrimination (see Fig. 1).69–71\n   271→Imprecision in production and perception can lead to the miscommunication of intervals (see Interval Spacing\n   272→Model, SI Section S3), and so intervals must be sufficiently large to be distinguishable. While IS theory predicts that\n   273→scale intervals should not be too small, MC theory predicts that they should not be too large (see Motor Constraint\n   274→Model), since large intervals are more costly to sing than small intervals. We combine these two theories into a\n   275→composite Melody theory that predicts an optimal step-size for scales that balances distinguishability and energetic\n   276→cost (see Melody Model).\n   277→Conflicting accounts have reported that symmetry (i.e., equidistance) in scales is either common12,72 or rare.2,73,74\n   278→While some explanations have been proposed based on certain mathematical properties of scales75,76 that may\n   279→facilitate learning of melodies and key-finding,23,73,77 these properties are tailored to Western 12-tone systems and are\n   280→ill-fitted to empirical measurements of scales. We instead view the question of symmetry through the lens of\n   281→information theory, and propose a bias towards low-complexity scales (see Complexity Model), which may lead to\n   282→lower melodic entropy78,79 or facilitate reliable manufacture and tuning of instruments.\n   283→In the current study, we used computational methods to systematically compare the major theories of scale evolution\n   284→using a global set of 1,314 scales from 96 countries, encompassing vocal, instrumental, and music-theoretic scales.\n   285→As shown in Fig. 1, we compared 1) a Melody model, 2) several variants of a Harmony model, and 3) a Complexity\n   286→model for their ability to predict both the empirical step-size distributions of the scales and the prevalence of\n   287→particular intervals in scales (e.g., the perfect fifth) cross-culturally.\n   288→\n   289→\f3\n   290→\n   291→Figure 1. Classification of scale theories into Melody, Harmony, and Complexity categories. Melody theories (left) are based\n   292→on pitch production and perception. The Interval Spacing model quantifies the fidelity of melodic communication using a\n   293→signal-detection framework. Intervals are imprecisely produced and perceived, and so if interval categories ‘A’ and ‘B’ are too\n   294→close in a scale, they can be misheard. The Motor Constraint hypothesis predicts that large melodic intervals are unlikely because\n   295→they cost more energy to produce. Harmony theories (right), including harmonicity and interference theories, are used to model\n   296→the perception of tonal fusion and beats. Their predictions for scales overlap and so we treat them together. Complexity theory\n   297→(bottom) posits that low-complexity scales enhance memory for melodies and facilitate instrument tuning and construction. In the\n   298→example presented, the simple scale (bottom left) has a small melodic vocabulary (i.e., one step-size,\n   299→sizes,\n   300→\n   301→, and three interval\n   302→\n   303→), while the complex scale (bottom right) has a large vocabulary (i.e., three step-sizes and five interval sizes).\n   304→\n   305→\f4\n   306→\n   307→=== 2409.11272v7.pdf ===\n   308→LOLA – An Open-Source Massively Multilingual Large Language Model\n   309→Nikit Srivastava1 , Denis Kuchelev, Tatiana Moteu Ngoli1 , Kshitij Shetty2 ,\n   310→Michael Röder1 , Hamada M. Zahera1 , Diego Moussallem1 , Axel-Cyrille Ngonga Ngomo1\n   311→Data Science Group, Paderborn University, Germany\n   312→1\n   313→\n   314→{nikit.srivastava, tatiana.moteu, michael.roeder,\n   315→hamada.zahera, diego.moussallem, axel.ngonga}@upb.de\n   316→2\n   317→kshitij@mail.upb.de\n   318→\n   319→arXiv:2409.11272v7 [cs.CL] 2 Feb 2025\n   320→\n   321→Abstract\n   322→\n   323→nomenon in which the ability of models to generalize across multiple languages diminishes unless\n   324→their capacity is significantly expanded (Conneau\n   325→et al., 2020)—means non-English speakers often\n   326→have access to inferior systems. Additionally, many\n   327→new models (Jiang et al., 2023; Achiam et al., 2024;\n   328→Dubey et al., 2024) are pay-to-use, require personal\n   329→information, or do not fully disclose training details, creating significant hurdles for multilingual\n   330→research.\n   331→To advance multilingual language modeling, we\n   332→introduce LOLA,2 a massively multilingual model\n   333→that follows a GPT-style (Radford et al., 2019)\n   334→decoder-only architecture with sparse Mixture-ofExperts (MoE) layers (Shazeer et al., 2017). MoE\n   335→architectures have shown strong performance on\n   336→learning the underlying structure of the data (Chen\n   337→et al., 2022) but their application in multilingual\n   338→LLMs remains underexplored. MoE models can\n   339→effectively increase model capacity with minimal\n   340→additional computational cost, offering the possibility of leveraging implicit clusters like language\n   341→family groups and playing a crucial role in addressing the challenges of multilinguality.\n   342→Language family groups, consisting of languages\n   343→sharing common ancestral roots, offer opportunities for enhancing language models. Despite\n   344→linguistic diversity, these families exhibit structural, syntactic, and semantic similarities (Rowe\n   345→and Levine, 2015) that can be exploited to improve performance across related languages. Our\n   346→goal is to leverage MoE’s strengths to exploit the\n   347→phylogenetic structure of languages and achieve\n   348→better prediction performance. In particular, the\n   349→shared and non-shared parameters of MoE-based\n   350→models offer a promising approach to mitigating\n   351→the curse of multilinguality by increasing capacity\n   352→while remaining compute efficient (Shazeer et al.,\n   353→2017). By exploiting language families, we aim to\n   354→\n   355→This paper presents LOLA, a massively multilingual large language model trained on more\n   356→than 160 languages using a sparse Mixture-ofExperts Transformer architecture. Our architectural and implementation choices address\n   357→the challenge of harnessing linguistic diversity\n   358→while maintaining efficiency and avoiding the\n   359→common pitfalls of multilinguality. Our analysis of the evaluation results shows competitive\n   360→performance in natural language generation and\n   361→understanding tasks. Additionally, we demonstrate how the learned expert-routing mechanism exploits implicit phylogenetic linguistic\n   362→patterns to potentially alleviate the curse of multilinguality. We provide an in-depth look at the\n   363→training process, an analysis of the datasets, and\n   364→a balanced exploration of the model’s strengths\n   365→and limitations. As an open-source model,\n   366→LOLA promotes reproducibility and serves as\n   367→a robust foundation for future research. Our\n   368→findings enable the development of computeefficient multilingual models with strong, scalable performance across languages.\n   369→\n   370→1\n   371→\n   372→Introduction\n   373→\n   374→Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown\n   375→tremendous capability across a diverse set of tasks\n   376→in recent years (Radford et al., 2019; Kaddour et al.,\n   377→2023). This progress has propelled research, with\n   378→many chat-based LLMs1 gaining popularity among\n   379→general users. However, concerns remain, particularly regarding their accessibility for multilingual\n   380→usage (Joshi et al., 2020) and open-source licensing policies (Liu et al., 2023). The number of\n   381→competent LLMs significantly decreases for languages other than English (Üstün et al., 2024). This,\n   382→combined with the curse of multilinguality—a phe1\n   383→\n   384→ChatGPT: chat.openai.com;\n   385→LLAMA: llama.meta.com;\n   386→Mistral: mistral.ai;\n   387→Gemini: gemini.google.com;\n   388→Deepseek: deepseek.com.\n   389→\n   390→2\n   391→\n   392→1\n   393→\n   394→Source Code: github.com/dice-group/LOLA;\n   395→Model Weights: huggingface.co/dice-research/lola_v1.\n   396→\n   397→\f2\n   398→\n   399→close gaps in current models—particularly for lowresource languages—by enhancing cross-linguistic\n   400→transfer learning.\n   401→Another important factor is the availability of\n   402→LLMs as a free resource, accessible for anyone\n   403→to use, modify, and redistribute without discrimination against any individuals or purposes. Many\n   404→popular LLMs that claim to be \"open source\" either withhold their training datasets (e.g., Mistral, Grok3 ), fail to publish their training code\n   405→(e.g., Llama, Grok), or do not release their inference code (e.g., Grok-24 ) (Spectrum, 2024). In\n   406→some cases, these models are released under licenses that are restrictive, discriminatory, or impose additional conditions (Liesenfeld et al., 2023;\n   407→Liesenfeld and Dingemanse, 2024). The artifacts\n   408→\n   409→=== 2409.15949v1.pdf ===\n   410→Beats of Bias: Analyzing Lyrics with Topic\n   411→Modeling and Gender Bias Measurements\n   412→Danqing Chen∗ , Adithi Satish∗ , Rasul Khanbayov∗ , Carolin M. Schuster, and\n   413→Georg Groh\n   414→\n   415→arXiv:2409.15949v1 [cs.CL] 24 Sep 2024\n   416→\n   417→Technical University of Munich, Germany {danqing.chen, adithi.satish,\n   418→rasul.khanbayov, carolin.schuster}@tum.de, grohg@in.tum.de\n   419→Abstract. This paper uses topic modeling and bias measurement techniques to analyze and determine gender bias in English song lyrics. We\n   420→utilize BERTopic to cluster 537,553 English songs into distinct topics and\n   421→chart their development over time. Our analysis shows the thematic shift\n   422→in song lyrics over the years, from themes of romance to the increasing\n   423→sexualization of women in songs. We observe large amounts of profanity\n   424→and misogynistic lyrics on various topics, especially in the overall biggest\n   425→cluster. Furthermore, to analyze gender bias across topics and genres,\n   426→we employ the Single Category Word Embedding Association Test (SCWEAT) to compute bias scores for the word embeddings trained on the\n   427→most popular topics as well as for each genre. We find that words related\n   428→to intelligence and strength tend to show a male bias across genres, as opposed to appearance and weakness words, which are more female-biased;\n   429→however, a closer look also reveals differences in biases across topics.\n   430→Keywords: lyrics · gender bias · weat · embeddings · bertopic\n   431→\n   432→1\n   433→\n   434→Introduction\n   435→\n   436→Disclaimer: Lyrics in our dataset may contain vulgar language reflected in BERTopic labels. This does not reflect the authors’ opinions.\n   437→Music is integrally tied with gender identity, where lyrics, melodies, and\n   438→performance styles can reflect and shape societal perceptions of gender roles,\n   439→stereotypes, and experiences [2, 13, 17]. Through lyrics, artists have a way of\n   440→expressing their emotions and discussing unique themes. While these themes\n   441→often span a wide variety of issues, they can also propagate dangerous stereotypes\n   442→and objectification [18, 21, 27, 28], pointing out the need to critically examine\n   443→these gender biases that can occur in lyrics.\n   444→Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques are well-suited for analyzing\n   445→song lyrics because of their textual nature [5], facilitating unique insights into\n   446→the topics and gender-related aspects. Previous research has indicated that word\n   447→embeddings [4], which depict words as high-dimensional vectors, can capture\n   448→linguistic biases associated with human biases [10, 26]. This inherent capability\n   449→of NLP can thus be used as an advantage to explore and measure gender biases\n   450→in song lyrics [6]. While there has been prior work done in identifying bias in\n   451→*\n   452→\n   453→These authors contributed equally to this work\n   454→\n   455→\f2\n   456→\n   457→D. Chen et al.\n   458→\n   459→lyrics [3, 5, 7], these studies mainly focus on comparing the bias that stems from\n   460→artist gender, leaving a research gap in examining the gender bias implicitly\n   461→present in the lyrics themselves.\n   462→Our research aims to build on this prior work by further combining topic\n   463→modeling with gender bias measurements to understand how gender bias in lyrics\n   464→varies thematically and across genres. Topic modeling aids in revealing the topics\n   465→present in a corpus, such as song lyrics in our case [23].\n   466→In this paper, we use BERTopic [20], an advanced topic modeling technique,\n   467→to take an initial look at the persistent themes in the lyrics in different genres\n   468→and see how these vary across decades. Our analysis provides insights into the\n   469→increasing sexualization of women in lyrics over time and the prevalence of profanity in rap music. Simultaneously, we utilize SC-WEAT analysis to quantify\n   470→the gender bias in the lyrics and assess the association of various target word\n   471→sets with gender-related attributes [10, 25]. Our major contributions are:\n   472→– Topic analysis, using a stratified sample, to determine cross-genre topics,\n   473→recurrent themes, and charting their historical development.\n   474→– Analysis of the prevalence and variation of the gender bias in lyrics across\n   475→topics and genres, using SC-WEAT scores.\n   476→\n   477→2\n   478→\n   479→Related Work\n   480→\n   481→Song lyrics are interesting resources to determine underlying social differences,\n   482→especially gender stereotypes and objectification [6, 9, 17, 28]. Prior research\n   483→in NLP has shown that word embeddings pick up on cultural and gender biases\n   484→inherently present in the data [8, 14, 26, 30]. In our paper, we quantify this gender\n   485→bias using an extension of the Word Embedding Association Test (WEAT),\n   486→the Single Category WEAT score (SC-WEAT) [5, 10, 12]. Betti et al. [5] and\n   487→Boghrati and Berger [7] use the SC-WEAT score to analyze the nature of gender\n   488→bias in lyrics and the differences across artist genders. However, we expand on\n   489→this approach by using topic modeling to identify popular and intriguing topics.\n   490→We then analyze the gender bias in the lyrics on a per-topic basis, aiming to\n   491→uncover how this bias may vary across different themes.\n   492→Topic modeling clusters documents to summarize or classify them. When applied to lyrics, it provides an effective method of identifying recurring themes [16,\n   493→23]. Topic modeling algorithms like BERTopic have previously been used in gender and social science research, with Wickham [29] using the algorithm to study\n   494→gender expectations on social media and their impact on suicidal ideation.\n   495→\n   496→3\n   497→\n   498→Experimental Setup\n   499→\n   500→3.1\n   501→\n   502→Data\n   503→\n   504→The dataset used for the lyric analysis is a combination of artist metadata from\n   505→the WASABI Song Corpus created by Fell et al. [16], and English lyrical content\n   506→from Genius Song Lyrics * . Our lyrics dataset includes data as recent as 2022\n   507→*\n   508→\n   509→https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/carlosgdcj/genius-song-lyrics-with-language-information\n   510→\n   511→=== 2410.03129v2.pdf ===\n   512→ARB-LLM: A LTERNATING R EFINED B INARIZATIONS\n   513→FOR L ARGE L ANGUAGE M ODELS\n   514→\n   515→A BSTRACT\n   516→Large Language Models (LLMs) have greatly pushed forward advancements in\n   517→natural language processing, yet their high memory and computational demands\n   518→hinder practical deployment. Binarization, as an effective compression technique,\n   519→can shrink model weights to just 1 bit, significantly reducing the high demands\n   520→on computation and memory. However, current binarization methods struggle to\n   521→narrow the distribution gap between binarized and full-precision weights, while\n   522→also overlooking the column deviation in LLM weight distribution. To tackle these\n   523→issues, we propose ARB-LLM, a novel 1-bit post-training quantization (PTQ)\n   524→technique tailored for LLMs. To narrow the distribution shift between binarized\n   525→and full-precision weights, we first design an alternating refined binarization (ARB)\n   526→algorithm to progressively update the binarization parameters, which significantly\n   527→reduces the quantization error. Moreover, considering the pivot role of calibration\n   528→data and the column deviation in LLM weights, we further extend ARB to ARB-X\n   529→and ARB-RC. In addition, we refine the weight partition strategy with columngroup bitmap (CGB), which further enhance performance. Equipping ARB-X\n   530→and ARB-RC with CGB, we obtain ARB-LLMX and ARB-LLMRC respectively,\n   531→which significantly outperform state-of-the-art (SOTA) binarization methods for\n   532→LLMs. As a binary PTQ method, our ARB-LLMRC is the first to surpass FP16\n   533→models of the same size. The code and models will be available at https:\n   534→//github.com/ZHITENGLI/ARB-LLM.\n   535→1\n   536→\n   537→I NTRODUCTION\n   538→\n   539→Recently, Transformer-based (Vaswani, 2017)\n   540→FP16\n   541→60\n   542→PB-LLM\n   543→large language models have shown impressive\n   544→66B\n   545→30B\n   546→66B\n   547→BiLLM\n   548→performance across various natural language proARB-LLMRC\n   549→55\n   550→6.7B 13B\n   551→30B\n   552→66B\n   553→cessing tasks. However, this unprecedented ca(Ours)\n   554→13B\n   555→pability is largely attributed to the sheer scale of\n   556→2.7B 30B 66B\n   557→50\n   558→6.7B\n   559→these models, which often encompass billions of\n   560→1.3B 13B\n   561→2.7B\n   562→parameters. For instance, open pre-trained Trans45\n   563→former (OPT) series (Zhang et al., 2022) includes\n   564→1.3B\n   565→30B\n   566→2.7B\n   567→various models, with the largest boasting 66B pa40\n   568→1.3B\n   569→6.7B\n   570→rameters. Similarly, the LLaMA family (Touvron\n   571→13B\n   572→1.3B 2.7B 6.7B\n   573→35\n   574→et al., 2023) features variants such as LLaMA3100\n   575→101\n   576→102\n   577→70B, showcasing even larger architectures. The\n   578→Model Size (GB, log scale)\n   579→substantial memory requirements for inference in\n   580→such large models (e.g., 150 GB memory for a Figure 1: OPT performance on 7 zero-shot\n   581→70B model) pose significant challenges for their Question Answering (QA) datasets. Our ARBdeployment on mobile devices.\n   582→LLMRC outperforms the same-size FP16 models.\n   583→Avg. Accuracy\n   584→\n   585→arXiv:2410.03129v2 [cs.CV] 10 Oct 2024\n   586→\n   587→Zhiteng Li1∗, Xianglong Yan1∗, Tianao Zhang1 , Haotong Qin2 , Dong Xie3 ,\n   588→Jiang Tian3 , Zhongchao Shi3 , Linghe Kong1†, Yulun Zhang1†, Xiaokang Yang1\n   589→1\n   590→Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 2 ETH Zürich, 3 Lenovo Research\n   591→\n   592→The study of compressing LLMs can be categorized into weight quantization (Lin et al., 2024; Frantar\n   593→et al., 2023), low-rank factorization (Zhang et al., 2024; Yuan et al., 2023), network pruning (Sun\n   594→et al., 2024; Frantar & Alistarh, 2023), and knowledge distillation (Zhong et al., 2024; Gu et al.,\n   595→2024). Among these, binarization, a specific technique within the realm of quantization, is particularly\n   596→distinguished for its ability to achieve extreme memory compression, reducing storage requirements to\n   597→as low as 1 bit. Given the substantial size of LLMs, some binarization methods adopt the post-training\n   598→quantization (PTQ) framework to enable a rapid transition from full-precision models to compact\n   599→binarized versions, requiring minimal resources (e.g., binarizing a 70B model in one 80 GB GPU).\n   600→∗\n   601→†\n   602→\n   603→Equal contribution\n   604→Corresponding authors: Linghe Kong, linghe.kong@sjtu.edu.cn, Yulun Zhang, yulun100@gmail.com\n   605→\n   606→1\n   607→\n   608→\fRecent binary PTQ methods, such as PB-LLM (Shang et al., 2024) and BiLLM (Huang et al., 2024),\n   609→emphasize the identification of salient weights, which are crucial for model performance (Lin et al.,\n   610→2024). The higher-bit representation and refined searching strategy for salient weights help to achieve\n   611→a better trade-off between performance and storage. Despite their success, the refinement of the\n   612→\n   613→=== 2411.04734v2.pdf ===\n   614→arXiv:2411.04734v2 [hep-th] 24 Dec 2024\n   615→\n   616→Decoherence of quantum superpositions by\n   617→Reissner-Nordström black holes\n   618→\n   619→Ran Li\n   620→Department of Physics, Qufu Normal University, Qufu, Shandong 273165, China\n   621→\n   622→E-mail: liran@qfnu.edu.cn\n   623→Abstract: Recently, it was shown by Danielson-Satishchandran-Wald (DSW) that for the massive\n   624→or charged body in a quantum spatial separated superposition state, the presence of a black hole\n   625→can decohere the superposition inevitably towards capturing the radiation of soft photons or gravitons [1–3]. In this work, we study the DSW decoherence effect for the static charged body in\n   626→the Reissner-Nordström black holes. By calculating the decohering rate for this case, it is shown\n   627→that the superposition is decohered by the low frequency photons that propagate through the black\n   628→hole horizon. For the extremal Reissner-Nordström black hole, the decoherence of quantum superposition is completely suppressed due to the black hole Meissner effect. It is also found that the\n   629→decoherence effect caused by the Reissner-Nordström black hole is equivalent to that of an ordinary\n   630→matter system with the same size and charge.\n   631→\n   632→\fContents\n   633→1\n   634→\n   635→Introduction\n   636→\n   637→1\n   638→\n   639→2\n   640→\n   641→Superposition experiment\n   642→\n   643→2\n   644→\n   645→3\n   646→\n   647→Decoherence from the entanglement photons\n   648→\n   649→3\n   650→\n   651→4\n   652→\n   653→Estimation of entangling photon number in Reissner-Nordström black holes\n   654→\n   655→5\n   656→\n   657→5\n   658→\n   659→Calculation of decohering flux\n   660→\n   661→7\n   662→\n   663→6\n   664→\n   665→Comparing the decoherence of Reissner-Nordström black holes with ordinary material systems\n   666→\n   667→9\n   668→\n   669→Conclusion and discussion\n   670→\n   671→11\n   672→\n   673→7\n   674→\n   675→A Calculation of “Coulomb” gauge potential on the horizon\n   676→\n   677→1\n   678→\n   679→12\n   680→\n   681→Introduction\n   682→\n   683→Recently, Danielson, Satishchandran, and Wald (DSW) demonstrated that the presence of black\n   684→hole Killing horizon can inevitably decohere the quantum spatial superposition of a charged massive particle [1–3]. This observation arises from the fact that soft photons or gravitons emitted by\n   685→a charged massive particle near the black hole can carry “which-way” information of the quantum\n   686→superposition.\n   687→This effect has attracted significant attention. In [4], a general formula for the precise decoherence rate was derived for the charged massive particle in the rotating Kerr black hole. However,\n   688→the calculations were limited to the scalar and electromagnetic analogs. To better understand the\n   689→thermal nature of the DSW decoherence effect, a model of an Unruh-DeWitt particle detector near\n   690→the Rindler horizon was investigated in [5], which indicates that the thermal environment can induce steady decoherence on the accelerating detector. In [6], by modeling black hole as a quantum\n   691→system at finite temperature, it was also shown that the decoherence phenomenon could also be interpreted in this context. Furthermore, it was analyzed that the decoherence effect caused by black\n   692→hole horizon is comparable to the analogous effect induced by ordinary matter system [6].\n   693→Based on the aforementioned works, the decoherence effect of a quantum superposition induced by a black hole can essentially be treated as environment-induced decoherence. This kind\n   694→of effect has been a subject of intensive research [7, 8]. Decoherence, along with the quantum\n   695→measurement problem, is closely related to the fundamental interpretation of quantum mechanics.\n   696→Although on the experimental side, it is difficult to generate spatial quantum superposition states\n   697→for large masses, there are some valuable explorations on this topic in the optomechanical systems\n   698→\n   699→–1–\n   700→\n   701→\f[9] and in Bose-Einstein condensates [10]. Therefore, the decoherence of quantum superpositions\n   702→by black hole is a particularly fascinating effect, and understanding the nature of DSW decoherence may provide intriguing insights into both the quantum nature of black holes and the black hole\n   703→information problem.\n   704→In this work, we study the DSW decoherence effect for a static charged body in the rn black\n   705→holes. Our calculation is limited to the case of electromagnetic radiation from the charged particle\n   706→in a spatial separated quantum superposition state. By calculating the decohering rate for the current\n   707→case, it is shown that the superposition is decohered by the low frequency photons that propagate\n   708→through the black hole horizon. In other words, we provide a detailed calculation demonstrating\n   709→that the quantum superposition is violated due to the entanglement between the black hole and the\n   710→charged particle, which arises from the black hole absorbing the entangling photon emitted by the\n   711→charged particle. We also discuss the case of the extremal Reissner-Nordström black hole. It is\n   712→shown that the decoherence of quantum superposition is completely suppressed due to the black\n   713→hole Meissner effect [11–14]. At last, we compare the decoherence effect due to the ReissnerNordström black hole with that of the ordinary material system and find that the decoherence effect\n   714→\n   715→=== 2411.16408v1.pdf ===\n   716→Low-Data Classification of Historical Music\n   717→Manuscripts: A Few-Shot Learning Approach\n   718→Elona Shatri, Daniel Raymond, György Fazekas\n   719→\n   720→arXiv:2411.16408v1 [cs.IR] 25 Nov 2024\n   721→\n   722→Centre for Digital Music\n   723→Queen Mary University of London\n   724→London, UK\n   725→{e.shatri, d.k.raymond, george.fazekas}@qmul.ac.uk\n   726→\n   727→Abstract—In this paper, we explore the intersection of technology and cultural preservation by developing a self-supervised\n   728→learning framework for the classification of musical symbols in\n   729→historical manuscripts. Optical Music Recognition (OMR) plays\n   730→a vital role in digitising and preserving musical heritage, but\n   731→historical documents often lack the labelled data required by\n   732→traditional methods. We overcome this challenge by training\n   733→a neural-based feature extractor on unlabelled data, enabling\n   734→effective classification with minimal samples. Key contributions\n   735→include optimising crop preprocessing for a self-supervised Convolutional Neural Network and evaluating classification methods,\n   736→including SVM, multilayer perceptrons, and prototypical networks. Our experiments yield an accuracy of 87.66%, showcasing\n   737→the potential of AI-driven methods to ensure the survival of\n   738→historical music for future generations through advanced digital\n   739→archiving techniques.\n   740→\n   741→I. I NTRODUCTION\n   742→The digitisation of historical sheet music is crucial for preserving musical heritage. Optical Music Recognition (OMR)\n   743→techniques convert these documents into machine-readable\n   744→formats like MusicXML and MIDI, allowing integration into\n   745→modern platforms. However, recognising symbols in these\n   746→manuscripts presents challenges due to document degradation\n   747→and variability [19].\n   748→Existing deep learning approaches to OMR typically rely\n   749→on large, annotated datasets, but the scarcity of labelled\n   750→data for historical documents presents a major obstacle [2].\n   751→Without sufficient labels, traditional OMR systems struggle to\n   752→generalise across diverse notational styles found in historical\n   753→archives. This paper addresses these limitations by proposing\n   754→a self-supervised learning framework that facilitates robust\n   755→music symbol classification with minimal labelled data, thus\n   756→supporting the broader goal of preserving and digitising musical heritage.\n   757→Building on the foundations of previous research by [15],\n   758→we hypothesise that a self-supervised Convolutional Neural\n   759→Network (CNN), paired with optimised classification algorithms, can effectively classify music symbols using only a\n   760→few annotated examples. A key focus of this work is optimising the preprocessing of musical ”crops”—small sections of\n   761→manuscripts—where we apply transformations that enhance\n   762→the model’s robustness to the varied forms of degradation\n   763→typically found in historical documents. The extracted features\n   764→are subsequently classified using methods like Support Vector\n   765→\n   766→Machines (SVM), multilayer perceptrons (MLP), and prototypical networks, ensuring accuracy despite limited sample\n   767→sizes.\n   768→Through experiments on historical music manuscripts, we\n   769→demonstrate the advantages of this self-supervised pipeline\n   770→over traditional supervised approaches.\n   771→II. BACKGROUND\n   772→As mentioned in Section I, the digitisation of cultural artefacts, such as historical sheet music, is an essential component\n   773→of modern efforts to preserve and share cultural heritage. However, this process presents significant challenges, particularly\n   774→in fields like OMR, where the diversity and degradation of\n   775→historical documents require more advanced approaches than\n   776→those used for modern printed music. Deep learning models\n   777→have proven effective in image classification tasks, but they\n   778→typically rely on large volumes of annotated data—data that\n   779→is often scarce for historical manuscripts [17].\n   780→Few-shot learning addresses this limitation by enabling\n   781→models to generalise from only a few training examples. This\n   782→is particularly valuable for tasks like OMR in historical contexts, where obtaining extensive labelled data is impractical.\n   783→The few-shot learning paradigm, often utilising an N-way-Kshot approach, empowers models to recognise and classify\n   784→symbols based on a minimal number of labelled samples [17].\n   785→Current OMR systems for modern, printed scores achieve high\n   786→accuracy through deep learning techniques such as transformer\n   787→[25] and object detection [22], [23] largely because of the\n   788→uniformity and well-annotated nature of the data. However,\n   789→when applied to handwritten or historical music manuscripts,\n   790→these models struggle due to variability in musical notation,\n   791→handwriting styles, and document degradation over time [19].\n   792→To mitigate these challenges, few-shot learning techniques\n   793→can be divided into two broad categories: metric-based methods and meta-learning approaches. Metric-based methods,\n   794→such as Siamese networks, project samples into a feature\n   795→space where similar items cluster together by minimising the\n   796→distance between them [18]. Prototypical networks extend this\n   797→concept by computing a prototype for each class, allowing\n   798→new samples to be classified based on their proximity to these\n   799→prototypes [9]. These approaches are well-suited for the task\n   800→of recognising symbols in historical music manuscripts, where\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"ae0e3b9c-38ab-489f-ab52-b2d3e26ffcb4","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:42.364Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b7da0da4-bc86-4a6b-bcee-5ff8819cc03c"}
