{"parentUuid":"23d6ff98-5f31-4a60-a174-9f31c98e002f","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_vrtx_01ALMUwZAy82Zi6qyACKydjD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01JZBsg7LzQcKth83zkopaWh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-root/cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01LMfCXLo7iMHKV6Xiuy588F.txt","offset":800,"limit":600}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":14,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14803,"cache_read_input_tokens":129301,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":14803,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXbDBeFXRMDVxmJXM386f","type":"assistant","uuid":"585b95ce-d007-4b44-9631-7ee06a8103f5","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:49.047Z"}
{"parentUuid":"585b95ce-d007-4b44-9631-7ee06a8103f5","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"cf5731bc-8cbe-4ac0-8743-cec986ed549e","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a654872","slug":"rippling-tumbling-bunny","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01JZBsg7LzQcKth83zkopaWh","type":"tool_result","content":"   800→of recognising symbols in historical music manuscripts, where\n   801→only a few labelled examples may be available.\n   802→\n   803→\fStage 2: Self-supervised\n   804→feature extractor\n   805→\n   806→Stage 1: Crop Extraction\n   807→\n   808→Crop\n   809→Distortion\n   810→\n   811→CNN\n   812→\n   813→Expander\n   814→\n   815→Crop\n   816→\n   817→=== 2501.07557v1.pdf ===\n   818→Decoding Musical Evolution Through Network\n   819→Science\n   820→Niccolò Di Marco1,* , Edoardo Loru2 , Alessandro Galeazzi3 ,\n   821→Matteo Cinelli1 , Walter Quattrociocchi1\n   822→1 Department of Computer Science, Sapienza University of Rome.\n   823→\n   824→arXiv:2501.07557v1 [cs.SD] 13 Jan 2025\n   825→\n   826→2 Department of Computer, Control and Management Engineering,\n   827→\n   828→Sapienza University of Rome.\n   829→3 University of Padova, Department of Mathematics, Padova, Italy.\n   830→* niccolo.dimarco@uniroma1.it.\n   831→\n   832→Abstract\n   833→Music has always been central to human culture, reflecting and shaping traditions,\n   834→emotions, and societal changes. Technological advancements have transformed\n   835→how music is created and consumed, influencing tastes and the music itself. In\n   836→this study, we use Network Science to analyze musical complexity. Drawing on\n   837→≈ 20, 000 MIDI files across six macro-genres spanning nearly four centuries, we\n   838→represent each composition as a weighted directed network to study its structural\n   839→properties. Our results show that Classical and Jazz compositions have higher\n   840→complexity and melodic diversity than recently developed genres. However, a temporal analysis reveals a trend toward simplification, with even Classical and Jazz\n   841→nearing the complexity levels of modern genres. This study highlights how digital tools and streaming platforms shape musical evolution, fostering new genres\n   842→while driving homogenization and simplicity.\n   843→Keywords: Network Science, Music, Evolution\n   844→\n   845→Introduction\n   846→Music is a defining element of human culture, reflecting and shaping traditions, emotions, and societal changes [1–3]. Its capacity to engage cognition and emotion has long\n   847→intrigued researchers across disciplines [4–10]. Over centuries, it has evolved alongside\n   848→\n   849→1\n   850→\n   851→\fcultural and technological shifts, adapting to new tools and contexts of creation and\n   852→consumption [11].\n   853→Historically, music was a communal experience, limited to live performances and\n   854→closely tied to specific cultural practices [12]. Moreover, composing music was restricted\n   855→mainly to trained specialists, often working within traditional frameworks. The invention of new technologies revolutionized this dynamic, allowing music to transcend\n   856→temporal and spatial boundaries [13]. By the mid-20th century, physical formats like\n   857→vinyl records and cassette tapes had democratized access to music and enabled a\n   858→broader audience to participate in music creation, laying the groundwork for today’s\n   859→digital era [14, 15, 15, 16].\n   860→This shift has fostered the emergence of new genres and innovative styles, challenging traditional notions of musical expertise and redefining creative boundaries [17].\n   861→Moreover, the recent advent of streaming platforms and social networks has reshaped\n   862→our cultural landscape [18, 19], including how music is consumed and produced [20].\n   863→Platforms like Spotify and YouTube not only offer listeners personalized recommendations but also act as hubs for discovering and promoting new artists, effectively\n   864→functioning as the “new radio” of the digital age [21–23]. Algorithms play a pivotal\n   865→role in shaping these experiences, tailoring music discovery to individual preferences\n   866→and thereby influencing listening habits [24–27].\n   867→However, this interconnected landscape is not without its drawbacks. Previous\n   868→studies have suggested that content circulating in fast, interconnected, and algorithmically curated environments is subject to simplification processes, as seen in the case\n   869→of song lyrics [28] and social media comments [29]. This raises an important question: is a similar trend occurring in the contemporary musical landscape? Addressing\n   870→this question is challenging due to the lack of a precise method for measuring musical\n   871→complexity [30–32].\n   872→To fill this gap, we build on the approach of previous studies [33], utilizing Network\n   873→Science tools to analyze musical compositions and examine how democratization and\n   874→digital connectivity impact musical complexity and diversity. In particular, we analyze a dataset of approximately 20, 000 MIDI files categorized into six macro-genres\n   875→[34], choosing to represent musical compositions as weighted directed networks where\n   876→notes are nodes and transitions are edges. This approach systematically explores structural differences across genres and offers a potential method for measuring musical\n   877→complexity and its trends over time.\n   878→The properties of the networks reveal that Classical and Jazz compositions exhibit\n   879→greater complexity and melodic diversity than other genres. Furthermore, unlike previous studies, our measures offer a detailed characterization of the topological and\n   880→musical features defining each genre, shedding light on the foundational elements of\n   881→their identities. Finally, a temporal analysis of our measures reveals a trend toward simplification in genres such as Classical and Jazz, which have reached complexity levels\n   882→comparable to those of more recently developed genres, showing lower complexity.\n   883→Overall, our findings suggest that the democratization of music composition and\n   884→the creation of highly interconnected environments for music sharing have contributed\n   885→to the emergence of new, less complex, and more homogeneous genres, while even\n   886→\n   887→2\n   888→\n   889→\fhistorically more complex genres like Classical and Jazz have experienced a decline in\n   890→complexity over time.\n   891→\n   892→Results\n   893→Genre-Specific Network Properties\n   894→Before the main analysis, we recall that our dataset contains ≈ 20.000 networks in\n   895→which notes are nodes and edges are transitions among them.\n   896→The first natural question is whether networks from different genres exhibit distinct properties. To explore this, Fig. 1 presents several measures calculated on our\n   897→collection.\n   898→Figure 1(a) shows the CCDF of all the edge weights appearing in each genre.\n   899→Notably, all distributions are heavy-tailed, but we observe that Electronic, Pop, and\n   900→Rock genres exhibit longer tails than Classical and Jazz.\n   901→Figure 1(b) illustrates the distributions of several network metrics grouped by music\n   902→genre, namely the number of vertices, density, weighted reciprocity, and mean node\n   903→entropy. See Methods for each metric’s definition and their musical interpretation.\n   904→Regarding vertex count, Jazz pieces stand out with a higher number of notes, while\n   905→other genres consistently exhibit fewer notes. Interestingly, all genres share similar\n   906→density values. This property may be tied to musical patterns that lead a note to be\n   907→connected only to a limited number of other notes.\n   908→For weighted reciprocity, more pronounced differences emerge: Rock, Pop, Hip Hop,\n   909→and Electronic music exhibit very high reciprocity, whereas Classical and Jazz show\n   910→lower median values and, in some cases, even anti-reciprocity. Musically speaking, this\n   911→suggests that Classical and Jazz may feature extended melodies with non-repeating\n   912→pairs of notes. Conversely, mainstream genres such as Pop or Rock may favor shorter\n   913→phrases or ‘hooks’ that can enhance a song’s memorability and, in turn, its popularity.\n   914→Finally, the higher entropy values observed in Jazz and Classical imply nearly\n   915→uniform transitions between notes. This indicates that, on average, the transitions\n   916→between a source node and its targets occur with similar frequency. Interestingly, this\n   917→points to the fact that musical connections are pre-established among harmonious\n   918→\n   919→=== 2502.21321v2.pdf ===\n   920→1\n   921→\n   922→LLM Post-Training: A Deep Dive into Reasoning\n   923→Large Language Models\n   924→\n   925→Abstract—Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed the natural language processing landscape and brought to life diverse\n   926→applications. Pretraining on vast web-scale data has laid the foundation for these models, yet the research community is now\n   927→increasingly shifting focus toward post-training techniques to achieve further breakthroughs. While pretraining provides a broad\n   928→linguistic foundation, post-training methods enable LLMs to refine their knowledge, improve reasoning, enhance factual accuracy, and\n   929→align more effectively with user intents and ethical considerations. Fine-tuning, reinforcement learning, and test-time scaling have\n   930→emerged as critical strategies for optimizing LLMs performance, ensuring robustness, and improving adaptability across various\n   931→real-world tasks. This survey provides a systematic exploration of post-training methodologies, analyzing their role in refining LLMs\n   932→beyond pretraining, addressing key challenges such as catastrophic forgetting, reward hacking, and inference-time trade-offs. We\n   933→highlight emerging directions in model alignment, scalable adaptation, and inference-time reasoning, and outline future research\n   934→directions. We also provide a public repository to continually track developments in this fast-evolving field:\n   935→https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Awesome-LLM-Post-training.\n   936→Index Terms—Reasoning Models, Large Language Models, Reinforcement Learning, Reward Modeling, Test-time Scaling\n   937→\n   938→✦\n   939→\n   940→•\n   941→\n   942→Claude3\n   943→\n   944→Mistral Large 2\n   945→\n   946→LLMs\n   947→\n   948→AlphaGo\n   949→\n   950→DeepSeek-R1\n   951→LlaMA 3.3\n   952→\n   953→GPT-O1, O3\n   954→\n   955→Chain-of-Thought\n   956→\n   957→Claude 3.5 Sonnet\n   958→Grok\n   959→\n   960→LlaMA 3.2\n   961→\n   962→Confidence Sampling\n   963→TRPO\n   964→\n   965→Gemini 1.5\n   966→\n   967→RLAIF\n   968→\n   969→EXAONE 3.0\n   970→\n   971→DistilBERT\n   972→ALBERT\n   973→MiniLM\n   974→\n   975→Search Against Verifiers\n   976→\n   977→REINFORCE\n   978→Vanilla PG PPO\n   979→OREO GRPO\n   980→RLHF\n   981→\n   982→Offline Batch\n   983→Behavior Cloning\n   984→Self-Critique\n   985→\n   986→Starling-7B\n   987→\n   988→Qwen\n   989→\n   990→ng\n   991→odi\n   992→Dec\n   993→\n   994→DPO\n   995→\n   996→Re\n   997→wa\n   998→rd\n   999→\n  1000→Off\n  1001→line\n  1002→Pol\n  1003→icy\n  1004→\n  1005→End-to-End\n  1006→\n  1007→Prompt\n  1008→Low-Rank\n  1009→Knowledge\n  1010→Distillation\n  1011→\n  1012→Parm. Efficient\n  1013→\n  1014→Adapters\n  1015→\n  1016→Best-of-N Search\n  1017→\n  1018→Tree-of-Thoughts\n  1019→Consistency Decoding\n  1020→\n  1021→=== 2503.12340v1.pdf ===\n  1022→SVD-LLM V2: Optimizing Singular Value Truncation for\n  1023→Large Language Model Compression\n  1024→Xin Wang\n  1025→\n  1026→Samiul Alam\n  1027→Zhongwei Wan\n  1028→Hui Shen\n  1029→Mi Zhang\n  1030→The Ohio State University\n  1031→{wang.15980, alam.140, wan.512, shen.1780, mizhang.1}@osu.edu\n  1032→https://github.com/AIoT-MLSys-Lab/SVD-LLM\n  1033→\n  1034→Compression Ratio Truncation Loss\n  1035→\n  1036→arXiv:2503.12340v1 [cs.CL] 16 Mar 2025\n  1037→\n  1038→Abstract\n  1039→Despite significant advancements, the practical\n  1040→deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs)\n  1041→is often hampered by their immense sizes, highlighting the need for effective compression techniques. Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)\n  1042→is a promising LLM compression technique.\n  1043→However, existing SVD-based compression\n  1044→methods fall short in reducing truncation losses,\n  1045→leading to less competitive performance in compressed models. In this work, we introduce\n  1046→SVD-LLM V2, a SVD-based LLM compression\n  1047→method that optimizes singular value truncation in SVD compression with two techniques.\n  1048→First, SVD-LLM V2 proposes to use theoretical\n  1049→truncation loss of weight matrices to assign a\n  1050→unique compression ratio to each weight matrix at different layers to accommodate weight\n  1051→redundancy heterogeneity. Second, SVD-LLM\n  1052→V2 proposes loss-optimized weight truncation\n  1053→to ensure that the truncated singular values result in a lower and more stable truncation loss\n  1054→in practice. We evaluate SVD-LLM V2 on ten\n  1055→datasets and five LLMs at various scales. Our\n  1056→results show SVD-LLM V2 outperforms state-ofthe-art SVD-based LLM compression methods. Our code is available at https://github.\n  1057→com/AIoT-MLSys-Lab/SVD-LLM.\n  1058→\n  1059→1\n  1060→\n  1061→Introduction\n  1062→\n  1063→Despite the outstanding performance Large\n  1064→Language Models (LLMs) exhibit in various\n  1065→tasks (Zhao et al., 2023; Gozalo-Brizuela and\n  1066→Garrido-Merchán, 2023; Wan et al., 2024b; Shen\n  1067→et al., 2024; Wan et al., 2025), the significant resources consumed limit their widespread accessibility (Wan et al., 2024a; Wang et al., 2024a;\n  1068→Zhou et al., 2024). Model compression (Zhu et al.,\n  1069→2023; Shen et al., 2025) is one effective approach\n  1070→to reduce resource consumption. To avoid resourceintensive retraining, LLM compression is often conducted in a post-training manner. Techniques such\n  1071→as LLM quantization (Yuan et al., 2024; Huang\n  1072→\n  1073→PPL\n  1074→\n  1075→SVD-LLM\n  1076→\n  1077→Homogeneous\n  1078→\n  1079→L = 0.8961\n  1080→\n  1081→P = 11.8\n  1082→\n  1083→SVD-LLM V2\n  1084→\n  1085→Heterogeneous\n  1086→\n  1087→L = 0.7351\n  1088→\n  1089→P = 8.01\n  1090→\n  1091→Figure 1: Comparison between SVD-LLM V2 and SVDLLM. We randomly select a weight matrix from\n  1092→LLaMA-3 8B and compare the normalized truncation\n  1093→loss and perplexity (PPL) under 20% compression ratio.\n  1094→\n  1095→et al., 2024), unstructured pruning (Frantar and Alistarh, 2023), and structured pruning (Ma et al.,\n  1096→2023; Ashkboos et al., 2024; Zhong et al., 2024)\n  1097→have been proposed.\n  1098→Low-rank approximation, such as Singular Value\n  1099→Decomposition (SVD) is also an effective technique for compressing LLMs. Compared with\n  1100→quantization and unstructured pruning, SVD compression is more hardware-friendly. Recently, a\n  1101→few SVD-based LLM compression methods have\n  1102→been proposed. At a high level, these methods\n  1103→all focus on reducing the truncation loss during\n  1104→SVD compression to reserve accuracy. Specifically,\n  1105→FWSVD (Hsu et al., 2022) reduces truncation loss\n  1106→by estimating weight importance and preserving\n  1107→more important weights. ASVD (Yuan et al., 2023)\n  1108→injects a scaling matrix to reduce the truncation\n  1109→loss but was not able to achieve theoretical minimum truncation loss at each LLM layer. SVDLLM (Wang et al., 2024b), on the other hand, fills\n  1110→this gap by proposing a whitening matrix that obtains theoretical minimum truncation loss at each\n  1111→LLM layer, demonstrating superior performance.\n  1112→Despite such advantage, SVD-LLM has two\n  1113→limitations. First, SVD-LLM applies a homogeneous compression ratio to all the weight matrices.\n  1114→This coarse-grained setup unfortunately overlooks\n  1115→the heterogeneity of weight redundancy across different LLM layers. Second, SVD-LLM utilizes\n  1116→Cholesky decomposition for weight truncation.\n  1117→However, Cholesky decomposition requires the ma-\n  1118→\n  1119→\ftrix being decomposed to be positive-definite, a\n  1120→condition that is challenging to fulfill in practice.\n  1121→Moreover, Cholesky decomposition introduces numerical instability throughout its iterative process.\n  1122→\n  1123→=== 2505.00035v1.pdf ===\n  1124→Linguistic Complexity and Socio-cultural Patterns\n  1125→in Hip-Hop Lyrics\n  1126→Aayam Bansal* , Raghav Agarwal, Kaashvi Jain\n  1127→\n  1128→arXiv:2505.00035v1 [cs.CL] 29 Apr 2025\n  1129→\n  1130→aayambansal@gmail.com, 2008raghavagarwal@gmail.com, kaashi6j@gmail.com\n  1131→\n  1132→Abstract—This paper presents a comprehensive computational\n  1133→framework for analyzing linguistic complexity and socio-cultural\n  1134→trends in hip-hop lyrics. Using a dataset of 3,814 songs from 146\n  1135→influential artists spanning four decades (1980-2020), we employ\n  1136→natural language processing techniques to quantify multiple\n  1137→dimensions of lyrical complexity. Our analysis reveals a 23.7%\n  1138→increase in vocabulary diversity over the study period, with East\n  1139→Coast artists demonstrating 17.3% higher lexical variation than\n  1140→other regions. Rhyme density increased by 34.2% across all\n  1141→regions, with Midwest artists exhibiting the highest technical\n  1142→complexity (3.04 rhymes per line). Topic modeling identified\n  1143→significant shifts in thematic content, with social justice themes\n  1144→decreasing from 28.5% to 13.8% of content while introspective\n  1145→themes increased from 7.6% to 26.3%. Sentiment analysis demonstrated that lyrics became significantly more negative during\n  1146→sociopolitical crises, with polarity decreasing by 0.31 following\n  1147→major social unrest. Multi-dimensional analysis revealed four distinct stylistic approaches that correlate strongly with geographic\n  1148→origin (r=0.68, p¡0.001) and time period (r=0.59, p¡0.001). These\n  1149→findings establish quantitative evidence for the evolution of hiphop as both an art form and a reflection of societal dynamics,\n  1150→providing insights into the interplay between linguistic innovation\n  1151→and cultural context in popular music.\n  1152→\n  1153→I. I NTRODUCTION\n  1154→Hip-hop, originating in the South Bronx in the early 1970s,\n  1155→has evolved from a localized cultural expression into a global\n  1156→artistic and commercial phenomenon [17]. The genre’s foundation lies in its lyrical complexity, with artists using innovative\n  1157→language patterns, intricate rhyme schemes, and narrative\n  1158→techniques to convey meaning and emotion. The linguistic\n  1159→richness of hip-hop makes it an ideal domain for computational\n  1160→analysis, offering insights into both artistic innovation and\n  1161→socio-cultural patterns [53].\n  1162→The computational study of hip-hop lyrics presents unique\n  1163→challenges and opportunities. Unlike traditional poetic forms\n  1164→with standardized structures, hip-hop exhibits tremendous diversity [72] in its approach to language, incorporating regional\n  1165→vernaculars, slang, metaphorical constructions [24], and culturally specific references. This complexity requires sophisticated analytical methods that can capture the multidimensional\n  1166→aspects of linguistic innovation while contextualizing them\n  1167→within their cultural framework [37].\n  1168→This study aims to develop and apply computational methods to analyze hip-hop lyrics across multiple dimensions:\n  1169→quantifying linguistic complexity through metrics of lexical\n  1170→diversity, rhyme patterns, and syntactic structure [3]; tracking\n  1171→thematic evolution through topic modeling and semantic analysis; correlating linguistic patterns with artist demographics,\n  1172→\n  1173→geographic origins, and temporal contexts; identifying relationships between lyrical features and commercial success or\n  1174→critical reception; and exploring how socio-political events\n  1175→influence thematic content and emotional tone [82].\n  1176→Rather than treating hip-hop lyrics as static texts, we\n  1177→approach them as dynamic cultural artifacts that reflect and\n  1178→shape their socio-historical contexts [97]. By integrating computational methods with cultural analysis, we provide a comprehensive framework for understanding the evolution and\n  1179→impact of the genre. Our research makes several significant\n  1180→contributions to computational linguistics, cultural analytics,\n  1181→and musicology, including: the development of specialized\n  1182→algorithms for detecting complex rhyme patterns specific to\n  1183→hip-hop [43]; creation of a standardized methodology for quantifying linguistic innovation in lyrical content [77]; establishment of a large-scale, annotated dataset spanning multiple eras\n  1184→of hip-hop; identification of measurable correlations between\n  1185→linguistic features and cultural contexts; and demonstration\n  1186→of how computational methods can enhance understanding of\n  1187→artistic expression [63].\n  1188→Figure 5 illustrates hip-hop’s dramatic rise from a niche\n  1189→genre to a dominant form of musical expression over the past\n  1190→four decades. This growth trajectory reflects not only commercial expansion but also increasing cultural legitimacy, as the\n  1191→genre evolved from localized expression to global phenomenon\n  1192→[19]. The substantial increase in Billboard entries represents\n  1193→both wider audience acceptance and the genre’s increasing\n  1194→diversity, providing an ideal framework for examining how\n  1195→linguistic patterns evolve as an art form moves from margins\n  1196→to mainstream [107].\n  1197→II. R ELATED W ORK\n  1198→The computational analysis of music lyrics represents a\n  1199→growing interdisciplinary field at the intersection of natural\n  1200→language processing, musicology, and cultural studies [81].\n  1201→Previous research has established foundational approaches for\n  1202→analyzing lyrical content, though comprehensive studies of\n  1203→hip-hop remain relatively limited.\n  1204→Early computational studies of lyrics focused primarily on\n  1205→genre classification and sentiment analysis. Fell and Sporleder\n  1206→[36] developed methods for automatic genre classification\n  1207→based on linguistic features, achieving 74% accuracy in distinguishing between music genres. Hu and Downie [45] explored\n  1208→sentiment patterns across musical genres, finding that hip-hop\n  1209→exhibited the highest variance in emotional content. These\n  1210→works established the viability of applying NLP techniques\n  1211→\n  1212→\fTABLE I\n  1213→S UMMARY OF P RIOR R ESEARCH ON C OMPUTATIONAL A NALYSIS OF\n  1214→LYRICS\n  1215→Study\n  1216→\n  1217→Methodology\n  1218→\n  1219→Fell & Sporleder\n  1220→(2014)\n  1221→\n  1222→Feature-based\n  1223→classification\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"39e0e110-b018-4735-a944-f72f43135d75","timestamp":"2026-01-29T06:39:49.064Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"585b95ce-d007-4b44-9631-7ee06a8103f5"}
